<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
        SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is DECLARED, having previously been stripped with tools:node="remove".

        It was removed on the reasoning that a reminder may be a few minutes late under battery
        management and that this was an accepted trade. On a real handset it was not: Android
        batches an inexact alarm with whatever else it decides to wake for, so "remind me at 2:20"
        arrives whenever Doze next relents. An owner who sets a reminder for a call at 2:20 needs
        it at 2:20.

        WITHOUT THIS LINE THE DOWNGRADE IS SILENT, which is the part worth knowing. The plugin
        defaults `isExactNotification` to true and then computes
        `useExact = isExactNotification && canScheduleExactAlarms()`
        (LocalNotificationManager.kt:357). On Android 12+ that second call is false unless this
        permission is both declared and granted, so the schedule call still succeeds and quietly
        becomes setAndAllowWhileIdle. Nothing throws and nothing warns.

        SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM rather than USE_EXACT_ALARM deliberately: USE_EXACT_ALARM is granted
        at install with no prompt, but Google Play restricts it to apps whose core purpose is an
        alarm clock or calendar, and a CRM is neither. This one is user-granted instead - Android
        shows it under "Alarms & reminders" in app settings, and the Reminders screen links
        straight there. Declaring it costs a listing nothing.

        If the owner later revokes it, Android restarts the app and drops every exact alarm it
        had scheduled. That is survivable here: the reconciler reads getPending() before it does
        anything, finds nothing armed, and re-arms on the next sync.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />

    <!--
        SET_ALARM lets Vistaar hand a reminder to the phone's own Clock app, so it rings on the
        alarm stream like any other alarm instead of chiming once in the notification shade.

        A NORMAL permission: granted at install, no runtime prompt, nothing for the owner to turn
        on. It does not let this app read or delete the owner's existing alarms - ACTION_SET_ALARM
        only creates, which is also why a reminder cancelled in the CRM cannot take its Clock
        alarm with it. See ClockAlarmPlugin.java.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.android.alarm.permission.SET_ALARM" />

    <!--
        SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW is here for ONE reason: it is the only way a closed app may create a
        Clock alarm. Read this before deciding it looks excessive.

        ACTION_SET_ALARM is an ACTIVITY action - the only alarm API Android has. There is no
        ContentProvider for alarms (contrast android.provider.CalendarContract, which exposes a
        CONTENT_URI you can insert into from a service; android.provider.AlarmClock is 23 String
        constants and nothing else). So creating a Clock alarm means startActivity, and Android
        10+ blocks that from the background unless one of eight documented exemptions applies.

        Of those eight, exactly one is reachable by an ordinary app: "the app has the
        SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW permission granted by the user". Receiving a high-priority FCM
        message is NOT an exemption. Holding SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is NOT an exemption. A
        foreground service is NOT an exemption.

        NOT GRANTED AT INSTALL, and nothing here grants it. It is a special permission the owner
        must switch on themselves under "Display over other apps", and the Reminders screen links
        there. Until they do, ClockAlarmArmer declines and the feature is simply off - every
        other reminder channel is untouched.

        THIS APP NEVER DRAWS AN OVERLAY. The permission is held solely for the background-launch
        exemption it carries; there is no overlay view anywhere in this codebase.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW" />

    <!--
        Android 11 filters which other apps a package can see. Without this, resolveActivity()
        for ACTION_SET_ALARM returns null even on a phone with a perfectly good Clock app, and
        the plugin would report Clock alarms as unavailable on every modern handset.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.SET_ALARM" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <!--
          windowSoftInputMode=adjustResize: the keyboard must RESIZE the window, not slide it
          up over the content. The dashboard is a full-height flex column whose last child is
          the message composer. Panned (the alternative), the keyboard covers the very input
          that was just tapped, and `100dvh` never changes because the viewport did not.
          Resized, dvh shrinks, the thread above gets shorter and the composer stays on screen.
        -->
        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
                ANDROID APP LINKS. Makes this app the handler for the `View lead:` and
                `View reminder:` URLs the server puts in WhatsApp notifications, so tapping one
                opens Vistaar instead of a browser tab asking the owner to sign in again.

                `autoVerify="true"` is what makes it silent. Without it Android treats these as
                ordinary web intents: the first tap raises a disambiguation sheet ("open with
                Chrome / Vistaar Leads"), and whichever the owner picks once becomes the default
                - which is how a link ends up permanently opening the browser. With it, Android
                fetches https://vistaarcrm.duckdns.org/.well-known/assetlinks.json at install
                time and, if this package and signing certificate are listed there, hands the
                link straight here with no prompt at all.

                VERIFICATION IS A SILENT FAILURE. If the file is missing, served as the wrong
                content type, served over a redirect, or lists a different signing certificate
                than the installed build, verification fails and Android falls back to the
                disambiguation behaviour above - nothing crashes and nothing is logged where
                anybody would look. `adb shell pm get-app-links com.vistaarverse.leadmanagement`
                reports the real state; see frontend/public/.well-known/assetlinks.json.

                THE WEBVIEW IS NOT NAVIGATED TO THIS URL. The shell is served from
                `https://localhost` (capacitor.config.ts); Android delivers the link as an
                intent and src/lib/capacitor-deep-link.ts reads the ids off it. The host below
                exists to route the intent, not to be loaded.

                No `pathPrefix`: both link shapes are the site root with a query string, and
                `android:path*` attributes do not match against a query anyway.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="vistaarcrm.duckdns.org" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <!--
            The FCM entry point, replacing Capacitor's.

            WHY REPLACE RATHER THAN ADD. Firebase dispatches a message to ONE service - the first
            that resolves for com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT. Declaring a second alongside
            Capacitor's would make which one runs a manifest-merge ordering accident. So
            Capacitor's is removed below and ours EXTENDS it, calling super on every callback:
            the existing push and token behaviour is byte-for-byte what it was, with the
            clock-alarm data message handled on the way past.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".VistaarMessagingService"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
            Removed so exactly one service answers MESSAGING_EVENT. VistaarMessagingService is a
            subclass of this very class, so nothing Capacitor does is lost - see above.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="com.capacitorjs.plugins.pushnotifications.MessagingService"
            tools:node="remove" />

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
</manifest>
